TAR(1)                                              ZX-UX

NAME
    tar, tap - save and restore files with cassette tape

USE
    tar cvf /dev/cassette/name file ...
    tar tvf /dev/cassette/name [file ...]
    tar xvf /dev/cassette/name [file ...]
    tap r file ...
    tap t
    tap x [file ...]

WHAT IT DOES
    tar saves files to tape. It can also list and restore them.
    A saved directory includes the files below it.
    Each file keeps its path, size, mode, and change time.
    Packed files remain packed on tape.

OPTIONS
    c   Write a new tape set. Start recording first.
    t   Show what the tape holds.
    x   Restore all files, or only the named files and folders.
    v   Print each file's kind, size, and path.
    f   Select the cassette name. Use at most ten characters.

    Use /dev/cassette without a name to read the next tape set,
    except that t (and tap t) then lists the whole tape: hold BREAK.
    Restoring a file replaces an older file of the same name.
    tar also makes any missing parent folders.
    If a file will not fit in memory, tar removes the partial file.

    tap uses the same code. Its r, t, and x commands match
    tar's c, t, and x commands.

TAPE LAYOUT
    Each tape set starts with a Spectrum header.
    Each file has a header and data blocks of up to 512 bytes.
    An empty header marks the end.
    Device files and resident /bin programs are not saved.

EXAMPLES
    tar cvf /dev/cassette/home /usr/home
    tar tvf /dev/cassette/home
    tar xvf /dev/cassette/home /usr/home/notes
